1. Schema injection on tools/list

The injection helper lives in [memory-api/tools/mcp/toolmon-policy-api/src/lib.rs](memory-api/tools/mcp/toolmon-policy-api/src/lib.rs#L51), in pub fn inject_caller_model_schema(tool: &mut Value) -> line 52-87. It is the code path that adds caller_model into each advertised tool schema.

    pub fn inject_caller_model_schema(tool: &mut Value) {
        let Some(obj) = tool.as_object_mut() else {
            return;
        };
        let schema = obj
            .entry("inputSchema")
            .or_insert_with(|| serde_json::json!({ "type": "object" }));
        let Some(schema_obj) = schema.as_object_mut() else {
            return;
        };
        schema_obj
            .entry("type")
            .or_insert_with(|| serde_json::json!("object"));

        let props = schema_obj
            .entry("properties")
            .or_insert_with(|| serde_json::json!({}));
        if let Some(props_obj) = props.as_object_mut() {
            props_obj.insert(
                CALLER_MODEL_ARG.to_string(),
                serde_json::json!({
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "Id of the model issuing this call (e.g. claude-opus-4-8). Required for price-awareness enforcement. Client-appended qualifiers such as 'Claude Sonnet 5 (copilot)', and space/underscore separators, are tolerated as a fallback and normalized to hyphens; prefer the exact price-table model_id."
                }),
            );
        }

        let required = schema_obj
            .entry("required")
            .or_insert_with(|| serde_json::json!([]));
        if let Some(arr) = required.as_array_mut() {
            if !arr.iter().any(|v| v.as_str() == Some(CALLER_MODEL_ARG)) {
                arr.push(serde_json::json!(CALLER_MODEL_ARG));
            }
        }
    }

The field is added under inputSchema.properties.caller_model, typed as string, and the helper appends caller_model to required if it is missing.

2. Validation on tools/call

The read/validate path is in [memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/proxy.rs](memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/proxy.rs#L177), in pub fn handle_client_message(mut msg: Value, policy: Option<&dyn Policy>, pending: &mut PendingList, pending_calls: &mut PendingCalls) -> (ClientAction, Option<CallTelemetry>) on line 177-182. The caller_model read itself is on line 203-209.

    let caller_model = params
        .get("arguments")
        .and_then(|a| a.get(CALLER_MODEL_ARG))
        .and_then(Value::as_str)
        .unwrap_or("")
        .trim()
        .to_string();

If the field is absent or blank, the code returns ClientAction::Respond(error_result(...)) and sets telemetry decision to reject-missing-model. There is no JSON-RPC error object with an error.code field here; the rejection is a normal JSON-RPC response carrying result.isError = true and a text content payload.

    if caller_model.is_empty() {
        let telemetry = immediate_telemetry("reject-missing-model", None);
        return (
            ClientAction::Respond(error_result(
                &id,
                &format!(
                    "Missing required '{CALLER_MODEL_ARG}' argument. Every tool \
                     call must declare the id of the model issuing it (e.g. \
                     claude-opus-4-8) so price-awareness enforcement can run."
                ),
            )),
            Some(telemetry),
        );
    }

3. Stripping before forward

The strip happens in the Allow arm of handle_client_message, in [proxy.rs](memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/proxy.rs#L277). The exact removal block is lines 277-285.

    Decision::Allow => {
        // Strip caller_model and grant_id before forwarding to the real server.
        if let Some(args) = msg
            .get_mut("params")
            .and_then(|p| p.get_mut("arguments"))
            .and_then(Value::as_object_mut)
        {
            args.remove(CALLER_MODEL_ARG);
            args.remove(GRANT_ID_ARG);
        }
        let decision_label = if soft_warning.is_some() { "allow-normalized" } else { "allow" };
        pending_calls.record(
            &id,
            PendingCall {
                tool_name: tool,
                caller_model: Some(caller_model),
                grant_id,
                decision: decision_label.to_string(),
                request_bytes,
                request_chars,
                started_at: std::time::Instant::now(),
                warning: soft_warning,
            },
        );
        (ClientAction::Forward(msg), None)
    }

4. Where the resolved value goes

After validation, raw caller_model is resolved through policy.resolves and then passed to policy.evaluate(&effective_model, &tool, grant_id.as_deref()). The resolved value is carried forward in PendingCall so the proxy can later correlate the forwarded request with the server response and emit telemetry in handle_server_message. The struct is in [proxy.rs](memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/proxy.rs#L75).

    pub struct PendingCall {
        pub tool_name: String,
        pub caller_model: Option<String>,
        pub grant_id: Option<String>,
        pub decision: String,
        pub request_bytes: u64,
        pub request_chars: u64,
        pub started_at: std::time::Instant,
        /// Soft warning to surface on the eventual server response when the
        /// `caller_model` only resolved after fallback normalization.
        pub warning: Option<String>,
    }

The budget/cost gate itself is the policy.evaluate call in handle_client_message, on line 268.

5. Request/response plumbing

The top-level dispatcher is the async fn run() loop in [memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/main.rs](memory-api/tools/mcp/mcp-toolmon/src/main.rs#L77), with the client-side routing loop on lines 192-239. main() just boots Tokio and calls run().

Order of operations for a tools/call:

- stdin line is read and parsed as serde_json::Value.
- handle_client_message(msg, policy.as_deref(), ...) decides whether to forward or respond.
- If telemetry is returned, it is emitted immediately.
- For ClientAction::Forward, the code records the JSON-RPC id as pending before writing to the child server.
- For ClientAction::Respond, the proxy writes the response straight back to the client.
- Non-JSON input bypasses the proxy logic and is forwarded verbatim.

